g('incoterms-2020-explained', 'inco', 'Incoterms 2020|the 11 terms explained',
  'The 11 Incoterms 2020 rules in one place: who pays freight, who carries risk, who clears customs, with a simple table.',
  'Incoterms are the standard trade terms from the ICC. They say who does what and who pays what in a sale, from the seller door to the buyer.',
  [('The 11 terms', [('table', ['Term', 'Mode', 'Seller delivers'], [['EXW', 'Any', 'At seller premises'], ['FCA', 'Any', 'To the buyer carrier'], ['CPT', 'Any', 'Carriage paid to named place'], ['CIP', 'Any', 'CPT plus insurance'], ['DAP', 'Any', 'At the named place, not unloaded'], ['DPU', 'Any', 'Unloaded at the named place'], ['DDP', 'Any', 'Cleared for import, duty paid'], ['FAS', 'Sea', 'Alongside the ship'], ['FOB', 'Sea', 'On board the ship'], ['CFR', 'Sea', 'Cost and freight to the port'], ['CIF', 'Sea', 'CFR plus insurance']])]),
   ('Where risk passes', 'Risk passes from seller to buyer at the point of delivery in the term, not when the buyer pays. It is the main thing to get right.'),
   ('Use the right group', 'FAS, FOB, CFR and CIF are for sea and inland waterway only. For containers, FCA, CPT and CIP are usually a better fit than FOB, CFR and CIF.'),
   ('Name the place', 'Always write a named place after the term, for example "FCA Delhi ICD" or "CIF Rotterdam, Incoterms 2020".')],
  [('Is an Incoterm a contract?', 'No. It is a set of terms you add to your sales contract.'),
   ('Which version is current?', 'Incoterms 2020. The older ones can still be used if you say so.'),
   ('Does the Incoterm change customs value?', 'Yes, because freight and insurance can be in or out of the price.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
g('fob-incoterm-explained', 'inco', 'FOB|Free On Board explained',
  'What FOB (Free On Board) means under Incoterms 2020: who pays what, where risk passes and when it is the right choice.',
  'Under FOB the seller delivers the goods on board the ship that the buyer names, at the named port of shipment. After that, the buyer carries the risk and the cost.',
  [('Seller does', ['Packs and marks the goods, and clears them for export.', 'Pays the costs to the port and loads the goods on board the ship.']),
   ('Buyer does', ['Books the ship and pays the main freight and insurance.', 'Pays the import duty and the costs at destination.']),
   ('Good for', 'Bulk and break bulk cargo loaded at the quay, and buyers who want to control the freight.'),
   ('Not good for containers', 'For containers, the seller gives the cargo to the terminal long before the ship loading. FCA is more exact because it hands over where the seller loses control.')],
  [('Who pays export customs?', 'The seller.'),
   ('When does risk pass?', 'When the goods are on board the ship.'),
   ('Does FOB include insurance?', 'No. The buyer arranges it.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
g('cif-incoterm-explained', 'inco', 'CIF|Cost, Insurance and Freight explained',
  'What CIF means under Incoterms 2020, who pays freight and insurance, where risk passes and the pitfalls for buyers.',
  'Under CIF the seller pays the freight and a minimum insurance to the named port of destination. But the risk passes to the buyer much earlier, when the cargo is on board at the origin.',
  [('Seller does', ['Clears the goods for export and loads them on board.', 'Books and pays the freight to the destination port.', 'Buys minimum cover insurance (ICC C) for the buyer.']),
   ('Buyer does', ['Takes the risk from the moment of loading.', 'Pays destination charges, import duty and clearance.']),
   ('Common confusion', 'CIF is a price term, not a delivery at the port of destination. If the ship is lost at sea, the loss falls on the buyer, who can claim from the insurance.'),
   ('Tips', 'Buyers should ask for wider insurance than the minimum if the cargo is valuable. Exporters should name the destination port, not just the country.')],
  [('Is CIF only for sea?', 'Yes, sea and inland waterway. For containers, use CIP.'),
   ('Who selects the carrier?', 'The seller.'),
   ('Is freight in the customs value?', 'Yes. India adds freight and insurance to the value of an FOB invoice, so CIF invoices already include them.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
g('exw-ex-works-incoterm', 'inco', 'EXW|Ex Works explained',
  'What EXW (Ex Works) means: minimum duty for the seller, maximum for the buyer, and why exporters in India should be careful with it.',
  'EXW is the lightest term for the seller: the goods are ready at its premises and the buyer does everything else.',
  [('Seller does', 'Makes the goods available at its place, packed and ready. It does not load the truck or clear export, unless it agrees to help.'),
   ('Buyer does', 'Collects, loads, clears export, ships, insures and clears import.'),
   ('The export problem', 'Export customs clearance needs the exporter IEC and a shipping bill. A foreign buyer cannot do that easily in India. So for exports from India, FCA with the seller clearing export is usually better.'),
   ('Where it works', 'Domestic sales and for experienced buyers with a local agent who can file the shipping bill in the seller name.')],
  [('Who pays the loading?', 'The buyer, unless the contract says otherwise.'),
   ('Can the seller export under EXW?', 'It can, with the buyer agent, but the seller stays the exporter on the shipping bill.'),
   ('Does EXW cut the seller risk?', 'Yes, from the moment the goods are available.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
g('fca-free-carrier-incoterm', 'inco', 'FCA|Free Carrier explained',
  'What FCA (Free Carrier) means under Incoterms 2020, why it suits containers and how the on-board bill of lading option works.',
  'Under FCA the seller delivers the goods, cleared for export, to the carrier or other party the buyer names. It works for any mode.',
  [('Seller does', 'Clears the goods for export and hands them to the buyer carrier at the named place, for example the seller own factory, or a container yard or ICD.'),
   ('Buyer does', 'Pays the main freight and takes the risk from the handover. Insures and clears import.'),
   ('Where to deliver', 'If the named place is the seller premises, the seller loads the truck. If it is another place, the seller delivers the truck there but the buyer unloads.'),
   ('On-board BL option', 'In 2020 the rules added an option: the buyer can instruct its carrier to give the seller a bill of lading marked "on board". That helps when a letter of credit needs it.')],
  [('Is FCA better than FOB for containers?', 'Usually yes, because the handover point matches where the seller loses control of the box.'),
   ('Who clears export?', 'The seller.'),
   ('Who pays terminal charges at the port?', 'It depends on the contract. Say it in writing.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
g('dap-delivered-at-place-incoterm', 'inco', 'DAP|Delivered at Place explained',
  'What DAP (Delivered at Place) means, who pays what and how it differs from DDP and DPU.',
  'With DAP the seller delivers the goods to the named place in the buyer country, ready for unloading. The buyer clears import and pays the duty.',
  [('Seller does', 'Arranges and pays the carriage to the place, bears the risk until arrival and does export clearance.'),
   ('Buyer does', 'Unloads, clears import and pays duty and taxes.'),
   ('DAP and DPU', 'Under DPU the seller also unloads the goods at the place. Under DAP the buyer unloads.'),
   ('DAP and DDP', 'The difference is import clearance: DDP puts import duty and clearance on the seller, DAP does not.')],
  [('Who pays the destination terminal charges?', 'Under DAP, the buyer in most cases, if the terminal is not the named place. Check the contract.'),
   ('Is DAP good for exporters from India?', 'Yes, if you control the whole route and can price the delivery.'),
   ('Who insures?', 'The seller bears the risk, so it should insure. The rule does not force it.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
g('ddp-delivered-duty-paid-incoterm', 'inco', 'DDP|Delivered Duty Paid explained',
  'What DDP (Delivered Duty Paid) means: the seller carries all cost and risk including import duty, and the risks of that for exporters.',
  'DDP is the term with the most seller duty. The seller delivers cleared for import at the named place and pays all the duty and taxes.',
  [('Seller does', 'Everything: export, freight, insurance, import clearance, duty and taxes, and delivery to the place.'),
   ('Buyer does', 'Receives and unloads the goods.'),
   ('Risks for the seller', ['It needs a way to clear import in the buyer country. In many countries only a local party can.', 'Duty and VAT surprises can hurt the margin.', 'Some countries make it hard for a foreign seller to claim input credit.']),
   ('When it works', 'E-commerce shipments with a landed price, and when you work with a local agent who is the importer of record.')],
  [('Who is the importer of record?', 'Under DDP, the seller should be, or pay for a local importer.'),
   ('Is DDP the same as door to door?', 'It is close, but check the named place and what the price includes.'),
   ('Can the buyer then claim VAT?', 'It depends on the country and who is the importer of record.')],
  ('landed-cost-calculator', 'Open the landed cost calculator'))
g('cpt-and-cip-incoterms', 'inco', 'CPT and CIP|carriage paid terms explained',
  'What CPT and CIP mean in Incoterms 2020: the seller pays the carriage, risk passes early, and CIP adds higher insurance.',
  'CPT and CIP are the multimodal versions of CFR and CIF. They fit containers and any mode.',
  [('CPT, Carriage Paid To', 'The seller pays carriage to the named place. Risk passes to the buyer when the goods are handed to the first carrier.'),
   ('CIP, Carriage and Insurance Paid To', 'As CPT, plus the seller buys insurance. In the 2020 rules the cover is the wider ICC A clause, not the minimum cover.'),
   ('Points to watch', ['Risk passes early, but cost goes to the named place, so a buyer can be at risk without insurance if not CIP.', 'The place named is the end of the seller costs, not where risk passes.']),
   ('Versus sea terms', 'CFR and CIF are for sea. For container cargo handed to the line at an ICD or CFS, CPT and CIP are the right ones.')],
  [('Is CIP more costly than CIF?', 'Slightly, because of the wider insurance.'),
   ('Who clears import?', 'The buyer.'),
   ('Can I use CIP for air?', 'Yes. It works for any mode.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
g('fob-vs-cif-which-to-choose', 'inco', 'FOB vs CIF|which should you choose',
  'FOB or CIF? How the two terms differ, who controls freight, how customs value changes, and which suits an exporter or importer.',
  'FOB and CIF differ on who books the ship and who buys the insurance. The risk point is the same: on board at the port of loading.',
  [('At a glance', [('table', ['', 'FOB', 'CIF'], [['Freight paid by', 'Buyer', 'Seller'], ['Insurance by', 'Buyer', 'Seller (minimum)'], ['Risk passes', 'On board', 'On board'], ['Who selects the ship', 'Buyer', 'Seller']])]),
   ('For an Indian importer', 'FOB lets you choose the forwarder and the line, and so control the cost and the destination charges. Under CIF, you rely on the seller choice.'),
   ('For an Indian exporter', 'CIF can win orders from buyers who want a landed price. It also lets you use your own forwarder rates, but you take the freight risk if rates rise.'),
   ('Customs value', 'In India, FOB invoices get freight and insurance added to reach the assessable value. CIF already includes them. The total should come close if the rates are real.')],
  [('Which is cheaper?', 'It depends on who has the better freight rates.'),
   ('Can the buyer change from CIF to FOB?', 'Yes, by agreement. Do it before shipment.'),
   ('Which is riskier for the buyer?', 'CIF, if the seller books a poor carrier or minimum insurance.')],
  ('incoterms', 'Open our Incoterms guide'))
